  "answer_text": "## Why Does `new_list = my_list` Affect the Original?\n\n### The Root Cause: References, Not Copies\n\nAssignment statements in Python do **not** copy objects \u2014 they create **bindings** between a target and an object. In other words, when you use the assignment operator (`=`) to \"copy\" a list by assigning an existing list variable to a new variable, you're not actually creating a copy of the list. You're just creating an **alias** that points to the exact same location in memory where the original list object exists.\n\nWhen you assign one variable to another, both variables are referencing the **same object** in memory, not two separate ones. This means both variables point to the same object via their references. When more than one variable references the same object, it's called a **shared reference**.\n\nAny modification to a shared mutable object via one of the variables that points to it **affects the other variable** that references the same object.\n\n---\n\n## How to Properly Copy a List\n\nGenerally speaking, there are two ways of making an independent copy of a list: a **shallow copy** and a **deep copy**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. \ud83d\udd2a Slicing (`my_list[:]`)\nThe simplest and fastest way to make a shallow copy:\n```python\nnew_list = my_list[:]\n```\nShallow copies of sequences (such as lists) can be made by making a slice of the entire sequence (`sequence[:]`).\n\n---\n\n### 2. \ud83d\udccb `list.copy()`\nA clean, readable built-in method:\n```python\nnew_list = my_list.copy()\n```\nThe Python `copy()` method returns a shallow copy of the list without taking any parameters.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \ud83c\udfd7\ufe0f `list()` Constructor\nYou can also pass the original list to the `list()` constructor:\n```python\nnew_list = list(my_list)\n```\nThis creates a new list object populated with the same elements.\n\n---\n\n### 4. \ud83c\udf0a `copy.deepcopy()` \u2014 For Nested Lists\nThe methods above all create **shallow copies**, which work fine for flat lists. However, although making a shallow copy of a list produces a true copy of the original list, any modifications to **nested elements** within it will be reflected in both lists. The reason is that the nested list in the copied list uses the same shared reference as the one in the original list.\n\nFor nested lists (e.g., a list of lists), use `deepcopy()`:\n```python\nimport copy\nnew_list = copy.deepcopy(my_list)\n```\nThe deep copy is different from a shallow copy in that the copied elements have their own pointers and are **not referenced** to the original elements. Therefore, no matter how you modify the deep copy, the changes will **not** be reflected on the original list.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcca Quick Summary\n\n| Method | Type | Safe for Flat Lists | Safe for Nested Lists |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `new_list = my_list` | Reference | \u274c | \u274c |\n| `my_list[:]` | Shallow Copy | \u2705 | \u274c |\n| `my_list.copy()` | Shallow Copy | \u2705 | \u274c |\n| `list(my_list)` | Shallow Copy | \u2705 | \u274c |\n| `copy.deepcopy(my_list)` | Deep Copy | \u2705 | \u2705 |\n\n> **Rule of thumb:** Use a shallow copy (e.g., `.copy()`) for simple flat lists, and `copy.deepcopy()` when your list contains other mutable objects like nested lists.",
